---
// privacy.astro — Privacy Policy (prerendered static).
// Modeled on gratisglobal.com's legal pages. Forward-looking: covers
// analytics, advertising, cookies, and user accounts ahead of launch so
// the policy does not need updating as those features arrive.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../consts';
import { ACCOUNTS_ENABLED } from '../lib/account';

const updated = 'October 11, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How NSN Explorer collects, uses, and protects your information, including analytics, advertising, cookies, and accounts."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This policy explains what information {SITE_NAME} collects, why we collect it, and the choices
    you have. It applies to our website at nsnexplorer.com, including any account features as they
    become available (together, the "Services").
  </p>
  <p class="mono updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>Plain-language summary.</b> You can browse {SITE_NAME} anonymously. We use analytics to
    understand how the Services are used, and we may show advertising to help keep them free. If
    you give us your email, we use it for the updates you asked for. An account is optional: if you
    create one, we store what is needed to run it, your email and your watchlists. We do not sell your personal information. You can contact us any time to access or
    delete your data.
  </div>

  <h2>Information we collect</h2>

  <h3>Information you give us</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Email signups.</strong> If you sign up for updates, we store your email address and
      the time you signed up. We use it to send you what you signed up for, and you can
      unsubscribe or ask us to delete it at any time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Account information.</strong> An account is optional. If you create one, we keep
      your email address, an account number issued by our sign-in provider, and your lists. See
      <a href="#accounts">Accounts and watchlists</a> below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Saved items.</strong> The parts, suppliers, supply classes, solicitations, agencies
      and searches you follow are stored on your account's lists so they work across devices.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Messages.</strong> If you email us, we keep your message and contact details to
      respond and to improve the Services.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Information we collect automatically</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Usage and device data.</strong> Like most websites, we automatically receive
      information such as your IP address, browser or device type, operating system, referring
      pages, the pages and records you view, and the dates and times of your visits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cookies and similar technologies.</strong> We and our partners may use cookies and
      similar technologies. See <a href="#cookies">Cookies and similar technologies</a> below.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we use information</h2>
  <p>We use the information we collect to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Operate, maintain, and improve the Services, including understanding which parts and records are most useful.</li>
    <li>Send you updates you signed up for.</li>
    <li>Provide account features you choose to use, such as watchlists and saved searches.</li>
    <li>Measure and analyze traffic and usage so we can make the Services more useful.</li>
    <li>Show and measure advertising that helps keep the Services free.</li>
    <li>Respond to your messages and requests.</li>
    <li>Protect the Services against fraud, abuse, and security threats, and comply with legal obligations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and similar technologies</h2>
  <p>
    Cookies are small files stored on your device. We use these technologies for a few distinct
    purposes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Essential.</strong> Needed for the Services to function, for example to keep you signed in if you create an account.</li>
    <li><strong>Analytics.</strong> To understand how the Services are used so we can improve them. See <a href="#analytics">Analytics</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Advertising.</strong> To show advertising and measure its performance. See <a href="#advertising">Advertising</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can control cookies through your browser settings. Blocking some cookies may affect how
    parts of the Services work. Where required by law, we ask for your consent before setting
    non-essential cookies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accounts">Accounts and watchlists</h2>
  {!ACCOUNTS_ENABLED && <p><strong>Accounts are not switched on yet.</strong> This section describes how they will work when they are; until then no account data is collected.</p>}
  <p>
    An account is optional and free. It exists so you can follow parts (NSNs), suppliers,
    manufacturers, supply classes, solicitations and agencies, save an Open-demand search, and see
    on your watchlist page what the public records have added since you last looked.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Signing in.</strong> Sign-in is run for us by
    <a href="https://workos.com/legal/privacy" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">WorkOS</a>. You sign in with a
    one-time code sent to your email address, or with your Google, Microsoft or GitHub account.
    WorkOS keeps your email address, your name and picture if the account you sign in with shares
    them, and a record of sign-ins (time, IP address and browser) to keep accounts secure. We never
    see a password.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What your account holds on our side.</strong> Your email address, the account number
    WorkOS issued, your lists and the pages and searches on them, any note you wrote on an item,
    whether you shared a list, and when you last marked each item reviewed (so we can show what is
    new). We do not record which pages you read while signed in. Our database provider stores this
    for us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cookies for signed-in visitors.</strong> Only when you sign in, the site sets three
    first-party cookies it needs to work: <code>nsn_session</code>, your encrypted sign-in session,
    which page scripts cannot read (30 days); <code>nsn_signed_in</code>, which only says that you
    are signed in, so pages know to ask for your watchlist (30 days); and
    <code>nsn_oauth_state</code>, which protects the sign-in itself and lasts ten minutes. If you
    press Follow before signing in, the page remembers it in your browser's session storage until
    you come back, then forgets it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shared lists.</strong> A list is private until you turn on sharing. A shared list can
    be seen by anyone with its link: its name and the pages on it, never your email address or
    account number. Turning sharing off makes the link stop working.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Deleting your account.</strong> "Delete my account" on your watchlist page removes your
    account, your lists and your WorkOS sign-in at once. Copies can remain in our database
    provider's backups for a short time before they are overwritten.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Email alerts.</strong> Not offered: changes appear on your watchlist page only. If
    email alerts are added, this policy will be updated first, they will be something you switch
    on, and every email will carry a working unsubscribe link.
  </p>

  <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We may use analytics services, including Google Analytics, to understand usage patterns such
    as which pages are visited and how long visits last. These services set their own cookies or
    identifiers and process data under their own privacy policies. You can opt out of Google
    Analytics using the
    <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Google Analytics Opt-out Browser Add-on</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Visitors in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom and Switzerland are asked before
    analytics loads, and it stays off unless they allow it. A Global Privacy Control signal from
    your browser is treated as a "no". Elsewhere analytics loads by default.
    <button type="button" class="copy" onclick="window.nsnAnalyticsChoice && window.nsnAnalyticsChoice()">Change my analytics choice</button>
  </p>

  <h2 id="advertising">Advertising</h2>
  <p>
    To help keep the Services free, they may display advertising. We work with third-party
    advertising partners, which may include Google and its advertising products. These partners
    may use cookies and similar identifiers to serve and measure ads, including personalized ads
    based on your activity over time and across sites.
  </p>
  <p>You have choices about personalized advertising:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Manage Google ad personalization at <a href="https://adssettings.google.com" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Google Ad Settings</a>.</li>
    <li>Opt out of personalized advertising from participating companies at the <a href="https://optout.aboutads.info" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Digital Advertising Alliance</a> and the <a href="https://optout.networkadvertising.org" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Network Advertising Initiative</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="suppliers">Supplier information</h2>
  <p>
    Supplier pages describe companies, not visitors. For each company that appears as the awardee on a government parts award, we
    show information from these sources:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Public government records.</strong> The company name and location from the public CAGE file, award records from DLA
      DIBBS and USAspending, and the registration data SAM.gov publishes about each entity: legal and trade name, business address,
      mailing address, registration dates, business types, NAICS codes and the government and electronic business points of
      contact (name, title and role only; we do not collect or show those contacts' phone numbers or email addresses).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The company's own website.</strong> Our crawler, NSNExplorerBot, reads up to four public pages of a company's
      website, obeys <code>robots.txt</code>, and records the phone number and email address the company publishes there.
      <a href="/about/#crawler">How the crawler behaves</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Automatic summaries.</strong> A short description of what a company makes is written by an AI model from those pages
      and is labelled as automatically written, with links to the pages read. It can be wrong, so each one points to the company's
      own site.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Correction and removal.</strong> If you represent a supplier, email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the CAGE code and say what to change. On request we hide a
    supplier's contact details (street and mailing address, points of contact, phone and email), its website summary, or both, and
    stop collecting them; hiding takes effect when the page next refreshes, within about an hour. Award records and the company name
    stay, because they are public government records.
  </p>

  <h2>How we share information</h2>
  <p>We do not sell your personal information. We share information only as described here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Service providers.</strong> Companies that help us run the Services process
      information on our behalf: our hosting and content delivery provider, our database
      provider, our analytics providers, our advertising partners, and, for accounts, our sign-in provider
      (WorkOS).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal and safety.</strong> We may disclose information if required by law, or to
      protect the rights, property, or safety of {SITE_NAME}, our users, or others.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Business transfers.</strong> If {SITE_NAME} is involved in a merger, acquisition, or
      sale of assets, information may be transferred as part of that transaction, subject to this
      policy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some advertising and analytics partners act as independent controllers of the data they
    collect through the Services. Depending on where you live, certain ad-related data sharing may
    be considered a "sale" or "sharing" of personal information under laws such as the California
    Consumer Privacy Act. You can exercise the choices described in
    <a href="#advertising">Advertising</a> and <a href="#your-rights">Your rights</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Outbound links</h2>
  <p>
    Every figure on the Services links to its official government source record, and we link to
    other third-party sites as well. Those sites can set their own cookies and collect information
    about you under their own privacy policies, which we do not control. We are not responsible
    for the practices of third-party sites.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights and choices</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access, correct, delete, or receive a
    copy of your personal information, to object to or restrict certain processing, and to opt out
    of targeted advertising or the "sale" or "sharing" of your information. To exercise any of
    these rights, email us at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. We will
    respond as required by applicable law and will not discriminate against you for exercising
    your rights. With an account you can review and change your lists directly, download a list as
    a CSV file, and delete your account and its data from your watchlist page at any time.
  </p>

  <h2>Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control</h2>
  <p>
    Some browsers offer "Do Not Track" or Global Privacy Control signals. Where required by law,
    we treat a recognized opt-out preference signal as a valid request to opt out of targeted
    advertising and the sale or sharing of personal information for that browser or device.
  </p>

  <h2>Data retention</h2>
  <p>
    We keep personal information only as long as needed for the purposes described in this policy,
    to comply with our legal obligations, resolve disputes, and enforce our agreements. Email
    signups are kept until you unsubscribe or ask us to delete them. Analytics and advertising
    data are retained according to the settings and policies of the relevant providers.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    We use reasonable technical and organizational measures to protect information, including
    access controls, least-privilege database credentials, and encryption in transit. No method of
    transmission or storage is completely secure, so we cannot guarantee absolute security.
  </p>

  <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The Services are intended for professional and business use and are not directed to children.
    We do not knowingly collect personal information from children under 13 (or the minimum age
    required in your country). If you believe a child has provided us personal information,
    contact us and we will delete it.
  </p>

  <h2>International users</h2>
  <p>
    We operate from the United States, and information we collect may be processed in the United
    States and other countries where we or our providers operate. These countries may have
    different data protection laws than your own. Where required, we rely on appropriate
    safeguards for such transfers.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy from time to time. When we make material changes, we will update the
    date at the top of this page and, where appropriate, provide additional notice. Your continued
    use of the Services after an update means you accept the revised policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy or your information? Email us at
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
